---
/*
 * /code-signing : the code signing policy SignPath Foundation requires
 * (signpath.org/terms). The footer links here as "Code signing policy" on
 * every page, which covers their "home page and download page" rule. Keep the
 * attribution line and team roles exactly true. Drop the "not signed yet"
 * paragraph once the first signed release ships.
 */
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { brand } from '../data/content';

const EFFECTIVE = '11 October 2026';
const maintainer = 'https://github.com/Slush97';
---

<Site title="Code Signing Policy · Grimoire" description="How the Windows builds of Grimoire get signed, who approves a signature, and where the privacy details live.">
  <article class="wrap policy prose">
    <p class="kicker">Effective {EFFECTIVE}</p>
    <h1>Code signing policy</h1>

    <p>
      Free code signing provided by <a href="https://about.signpath.io/">SignPath.io</a>,
      certificate by <a href="https://signpath.org/">SignPath Foundation</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Signing starts with the first release after SignPath Foundation accepts the project. Until
      then the Windows builds are unsigned, and Windows warns about an unknown publisher the first
      time you run one.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets signed</h2>
    <p>
      The Windows builds: the installer (<code>Grimoire-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code>), the
      portable <code>Grimoire-Portable-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code>, and the <code>Grimoire.exe</code>
      inside them. GitHub Actions builds them from a tagged commit in
      <a href={brand.github}>Slush97/grimoire</a>, then hands them to SignPath. Nothing built on
      someone's own machine gets signed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The installer also carries <code>vpkmerge.exe</code>, the open source tool Grimoire uses to
      read and write game files. GitHub Actions builds it from
      <a href="https://github.com/Slush97/vpkmerge">Slush97/vpkmerge</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Linux and macOS builds aren't covered here. Every release ships a <code>SHA256SUMS</code>
      file and build provenance attestations for all platforms, and the
      <a href="/download">download page</a> shows how to check them.
    </p>

    <h2>Who can sign</h2>
    <p>Grimoire has one maintainer, who holds every role.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Committers and reviewers:</strong> <a href={maintainer}>Slush97</a>. Changes from
        anyone else get reviewed before they're merged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Approvers:</strong> <a href={maintainer}>Slush97</a>. Every release is approved by
        hand before SignPath signs it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Grimoire has no telemetry. Every request the app can make, who receives it, and how to turn it
      off is on <a href="/docs/privacy">what leaves your machine</a>, along with links to the
      privacy policies of the services it talks to. This website has
      <a href="/privacy">its own privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Checking a signature</h2>
    <p>
      Right-click the <code>.exe</code>, open <strong>Properties</strong>, then the
      <strong>Digital Signatures</strong> tab. A signed Grimoire build lists SignPath Foundation
      as the signer.
    </p>

    <h2>Something looks wrong?</h2>
    <p>
      If a signed file doesn't match a release, or you think this policy was broken, open an issue
      on <a href={`${brand.github}/issues`}>GitHub</a> or say so in the
      <a href={brand.discord}>Discord</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Site>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 808px;
    padding-top: 72px;
    padding-bottom: 120px;
  }
  .policy h1 {
    font-size: 64px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 719px) {
    .policy {
      padding-top: 48px;
      padding-bottom: 80px;
    }
    .policy h1 {
      font-size: 48px;
    }
  }
</style>
